-- title:
--   Nakayama: local generation of J at a prime
-- statement:
--   Let $P$ be a commutative Noetherian ring, $J$ an ideal of $P$, and $\mathfrak{n}$ a prime ideal of $P$. Let $\iota$ be a type and $h : \iota \to P$ a family of elements of $P$ such that $h(i) \in J$ for every $i$, and suppose that $J$ is contained in the sum of the ideal generated by the range of $h$ and the product ideal $\mathfrak{n} J$, that is $J \le \operatorname{span}(\operatorname{range} h) \sqcup \mathfrak{n} \cdot J$. The conclusion is that the ideal of the localisation $P_{\mathfrak{n}} =$ `Localization.AtPrime 𝔫` generated by the image of $J$ under the structure map $P \to P_{\mathfrak{n}}$ coincides with the ideal generated by the set of images $\{\,\bar h(i) : i \in \iota\,\}$ of the family $h$. No finiteness is assumed on the index type $\iota$, and $\mathfrak{n}$ need not be maximal in $P$; the Noetherian hypothesis on $P$ supplies the finite generation needed for Nakayama's lemma.
--
--   This is Nakayama's lemma in the form controlling the local generators of an ideal: a family of elements of $J$ generating $J$ modulo $\mathfrak{n}J$ generates $J$ after localisation at $\mathfrak{n}$. It is used in the construction of presentations of ideals over local rings, being cited by the statement producing a square presentation of the kernel of a surjection with surjective induced map on residue fields.
